llink - devsys's fork of rlink, renamed llink on 2026-10-08 (D42) so it
never shares a name with the original. Forked into devsys on 2026-10-02 from ~/projects/dcc/mw-rlink:
upstream https://gitlab.com/debmint/mw-rlink commit 0c6de25 (the CoCo-native
source) plus the owner's uncommitted Linux port in that working tree
(GNUmakefile, rof.h, types.h, misc.c, util.c, ...). No upstream history kept.
Build: make -f GNUmakefile (the 6809 build: ../native/GNUmakefile).
Internal file names (rlink.h, rlink_main.c, ...) are kept, as las kept rma's.

Changes:
  phase 5  names up to 63 characters (SYMLEN, was 9 and silently cut);
           --lwlink: lwlink's module layout (rl_03.c, D14); grab() zeroes
           memory; fixed a format-string fprintf, a 16-character psect name
           read into 10 bytes, a null dereference in the unresolved list
  phase 3b --lwlink chooses library members and orders modules as lwlink
           does (lwsel.c, D17)
  phase 6  names from ROF version 0 objects match on 8 characters, with
           aliases (localtim -> localtime, D19)
  phase 7  K&R declarations (proto.h) so DCC compiles it; lwsel members
           renamed (one namespace for struct members in K&R C); no atexit
           or ftruncate on OS-9 (lw_cleanup called explicitly, _ss_size);
           names stored at their own length (setname, D23), ext_ref entries
           at least dref_node size (they are reused as dref_nodes)
  phase 8  lwsel's 14K module table allocated only for --lwlink (D29)
  phase 10 native --lwlink links fit (D31): lwsel skips constants-only
           members' names, grows its module table, frees its index;
           grab() uses malloc on OS-9 too; setname frees a replaced name.
           _bufsiz set only before a FILE slot has its buffer (CoCoC keeps
           the first one through freopen; 1,024 on a 256-byte buffer
           overwrote OutFile's)
  2026-10-08 renamed llink (D42); with neither --lwlink nor the new --mw
           it picks the layout from the mainline: lwlink's if the module
           with a type/language exports __lwsec (las's lwasm-style objects),
           else Microware's (lw_detect, rlink_main.c)

Design and roadmap: ../docs/DESIGN.md
